  "name": "Description of Prince William Sound Shoreline Habitats Associated with Biological Communities",
  "description": "This report references population abundance data of algal species: Fucus distichus, Laminaria groenlandica, and Zostera marina and invertebrate species: Clinocardium nuttali, Evasterias troschelii, Littorina scutulata, Littorina sitkana, Macoma balthica, Mya arenaria, Mytilus trossulus, Pisaster ochraceus, Protothaca staminea, Pycnopodia helianthoides, Saxidomus giganteus, and Strongylocentrotus droebachiensis and species composition data of fish species: Ammodytes hexapterus, Hexagrammos decagrammus, and Pholis laeta from Prince William Sound, Alaska. Referenced areas of Prince William Sound include: Bass Harbor, Bay of Isles, Bomb Point, Bomb Point Cove, Chicken Island, Disk Island, Eleanor Island, Elrington Island, Green Island, Hartney Bay, Latouche Island, Little Smith Island, Peak Island, Port Gravian, Sheep Bay, Sheep Bay Point, Simpson Bay, Smith Island, Storey Island, and Zaikof Bay. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Rosenthal, R. J., D. C. Lees, and D. J. Maiero. 1982. Description of Prince William Sound shoreline habitats associated with biological communities. Final report to the Department of Commerce, NOAA, Office of Pollution Assessment, Juneau Alaska. 59 p. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #842 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
